  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>An apartment balcony garden is completely doable, even on a small, partly shaded balcony. The keys are matching plants to the light you actually get, using lightweight containers with good drainage, growing vertically to save floor space, and watering more often than you would in the ground. Here is how to plan, plant and maintain a mini garden that stays within your lease.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Assess your sun, space and rules</h2>
<h3>Sunlight</h3>
<p>Watch your balcony for a full day and count hours of direct sun. In general, south- and west-facing balconies get the most light and suit tomatoes, peppers and sun-loving herbs. East-facing balconies get gentler morning sun, great for leafy greens. North-facing balconies favor shade lovers like ferns, mint and parsley. In Texas, a west-facing balcony can get brutally hot in summer afternoons, so plan for some shade cloth or heat-tolerant plants.</p>
<h3>Size and layout</h3>
<p>Measure the floor, railing length and wall height. Even a narrow balcony can hold railing planters, a hanging basket and a tiered stand.</p>
<h3>Weight and lease rules</h3>
<p>Wet soil is heavy. Ask your property manager about weight limits and rules on hanging items over the railing, drilling into walls, and water draining onto lower balconies. Keep the heaviest pots near the building wall, not at the edge.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Choose containers and soil</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Lightweight materials.</strong> Plastic, resin, fabric grow bags and fiberglass weigh far less than terra cotta or ceramic.</li>
<li><strong>Drainage holes.</strong> Non-negotiable. Without them, roots sit in water and rot.</li>
<li><strong>Saucers or trays.</strong> Protect the balcony floor and keep runoff from dripping onto your neighbors below.</li>
<li><strong>Potting mix, not garden soil.</strong> Container mix is lighter and drains better. Adding perlite or coconut coir can improve aeration.</li>
</ul>
<p>Planters that hang over the railing without screws are perfect for renters. A well-reviewed [[amz:balcony_planter|railing planter]] adds growing space without using any floor.</p>
<!--products:balcony_planter,grow_light,outdoor_rug|Balcony garden starter picks-->

<h2>Step 3: Pick plants that fit your balcony</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Light</th><th>Good choices</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Full sun (6+ hours)</td><td>Cherry tomatoes, peppers, basil, rosemary, thyme, marigolds, succulents</td></tr>
<tr><td>Part sun (3-6 hours)</td><td>Lettuce, spinach, kale, parsley, chives, mint, strawberries, pansies</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shade (under 3 hours)</td><td>Ferns, pothos, caladiums, impatiens, mint</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Choose dwarf, patio or compact varieties of vegetables, which are bred for containers. Vining plants like jasmine, sweet peas or pole beans can climb a trellis and double as a privacy screen.</p>
<p>In much of Texas, timing matters as much as plant choice. Cool-season crops like lettuce and spinach usually do better in fall and early spring, while peppers and heat-tolerant herbs can handle summer. Your county extension office (Texas A&amp;M AgriLife Extension) publishes local planting calendars worth checking.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Go vertical</h2>
<p>When floor space is tight, grow up:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Railing planters</strong> along the full length of the rail.</li>
<li><strong>Tiered plant stands</strong> or ladder shelves that hold several pots in one footprint.</li>
<li><strong>Freestanding trellises</strong> for climbing vegetables and vines, no wall anchors needed.</li>
<li><strong>Hanging baskets</strong> from a ceiling hook if your balcony has one and management allows it.</li>
<li><strong>Pocket planters</strong> hung from removable outdoor hooks for herbs and succulents.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Put your most-used herbs closest to the door. If basil and chives are within arm's reach of the kitchen, you will actually use them, and you will notice right away when they need water.</p></div>

<h2>Step 5: Make it a place you want to sit</h2>
<p>A garden you enjoy is a garden you take care of. Add a compact bistro set or folding chair, an [[amz:outdoor_rug|outdoor rug]] to soften concrete, and solar or plug-in [[amz:string_lights|string lights]] for evenings. Bamboo or fabric privacy screens attached with zip ties can block a neighbor's view without hardware.</p>
<p>If your balcony is too shady for herbs, bring some indoors under a small [[amz:grow_light|grow light]] near a window. It is also a great way to start seedlings before moving them outside.</p>

<h2>Step 6: Keep it thriving</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Water often.</strong> In summer heat, check soil every day. Water deeply until it drains, preferably in the morning.</li>
<li><strong>Feed regularly.</strong> Container plants use up nutrients quickly. A balanced fertilizer every few weeks during the growing season is a common approach; follow the label.</li>
<li><strong>Watch for pests.</strong> Aphids and spider mites are common. Spray them off with water, and consider insecticidal soap or neem oil if needed.</li>
<li><strong>Prune and harvest.</strong> Pinch herbs often to keep them bushy and remove yellow or dead leaves.</li>
<li><strong>Protect from extremes.</strong> Shade cloth in July and August; bring tender plants inside or cover them before a freeze.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Why a balcony garden is worth the effort</h2>
<p>Fresh herbs for cooking, a calmer spot to unwind and a real sense of accomplishment are all worth it. Being around plants is widely associated with feeling more relaxed, and growing even a few herbs can cut down on small grocery purchases.</p>
<p>For readers renting while they save for or plan a home build, a balcony garden is also a low-risk way to learn what you enjoy growing and how sun orientation affects outdoor space. Those lessons come in handy when you are <a href="/choosing-the-right-location-for-your-texas-dream-home/">choosing the right location and lot</a> for your future home, and when you weigh <a href="/the-pros-and-cons-of-building-in-urban-vs-rural-texas/">building in urban vs. rural Texas</a>, where yard size can be very different.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Start with three to five plants you will actually use, a couple of railing planters and a watering routine. To dress up the space for the cooler months, see our <a href="/fall-decorating-tips-for-your-apartment-balcony/">fall balcony decorating tips</a>. Ready to think about a yard of your own? Our <a href="/free-training/">free training</a> walks through how to build a new home.</p>
